@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+# v39 — faster ADB screenshots
+
+v38 started a host ADB command and compressed/transferred a full PNG for every
+screenshot. That overhead was repeated many times per camera view. v39 keeps one
+request-driven Android screen connection open, including foreground-app queries.
+Each screenshot is taken after its request; queued/stale frames are never used.
+
+At connection startup it warms up and measures PNG and uncompressed screenshots
+on the selected emulator, including transfer and decoding, then uses the faster
+format. Android raw RGB/RGBA/RGBX/BGRA/RGB565 images and both header layouts are
+handled explicitly. Unsupported raw layouts select persistent PNG. Whole-image
+black-screen checking now uses exact OpenCV statistics without allocating a large
+floating-point array on every capture.
+
+The route, map validation, loading/inspection waits, three-hit confirmation,
+20-second collection wait, return point and histories stay the same. Sniper and
+Spy keep Windows mouse/focus free. No new dependency, APK or root is required.
+ADB errors never fall back to Windows mouse input. Minimized operation still
+requires BlueStacks to keep rendering and the game to stay active inside Android.
+
+Exit the old app, extract the full package and run LoongLens.exe (header v39).
+Keep Android Debug Bridge enabled at the existing 127.0.0.1:5555 address. In
+Sniper Settings, Test connection / preview with the large map open; the result
+now includes measured capture milliseconds. Test map pan with BlueStacks
+minimized, then Save. Existing device/alignment, routes and history are retained.
+
+Windows checks compare the actual v38/v39 capture code with an independent
+simulated Android subprocess, modeled command/encoding costs and real transfer/
+decoding. They require fresh frames and zero new host processes per v39 capture.
+Native source/frozen GUI checks also verify this reuse and unchanged Windows
+cursor/focus. Linux runs the actual quoted Android shell script against command
+stubs, including binary framing, file permissions and cleanup. These checks are
+not a measured live BlueStacks speed claim; compare your actual route timings.
+
